ement of  the technology that i mentioned is muse  is really simon and joe that drove that  forward  the hyperspectral phaser really owes  its creation to francesco true trolley  and all of his colleagues  who've been working on different aspects  of improving the performance  and the speed and  of the hyperspectral phaser approaches  and integrating them  in with lifetime tools as well  the two photon spins really been driven  by tai  and his colleagues and i've listed a few  of them here but of course  in any one of these lists there's always  more that we could list  and finally the spectrophone has really  been something that has involved  many labs and as we've started to show  the technologies  we've found people in ophthalmology and  cancer biology  and beta cell biology and a variety of  other  systems these collaborating labs shown  at the bottom  that are working together with the  people in our lab to really refine  together these spectrophony sort of  techniques  and i think i'll stop there and see if  there's any questions and  hopefully you've enjoyed what i've  shared with you today  thank you dr fraser for your informative  presentation  and we want to thank our audience for  their interesting questions  all questions that come in today and  those submitted during the on-demand  period will be answered by dr fraser via  the email address you provided at the  time of registration  we would like to once again thank dr  fraser for his time today  and his important research we would also  like to thank labyrinth  and our sponsor leica microsystems for  underwriting today's educational webcast  you can view the webinar on demand lab  roots will alert you via email when it's  available for replay  that's all for now until next time  goodbye
 

